// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `f` in a call to `inner`
// A GENERIC FUNCTION CANNOT PASS ITS OWN GENERIC-TYPED FUNCTION PARAMETER TO ANOTHER
// GENERIC FUNCTION. No std involved — this is the whole defect in five lines.
//
// It is pinned here, under `std/`, because it is the reason `std:array` ships the SAME
// sixty-line merge twice. `sorted` should be `copy; sort(out, less); return out`; that
// call is this program, so it does not compile, so the body is duplicated and
// `array-sort-agrees.vl` exists to keep the two copies honest. When this fixture starts
// failing, `sorted` collapses to four lines and that gate becomes redundant.
//
// FOUR SPELLINGS WERE TRIED. All four fail, and the two distinct messages are worth
// recording because they suggest two different gaps rather than one:
//
//   inner(xs, f)                     monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `f`
//                                    in a call to `inner`                  <- this file
//   xs.inner(f)                      emitProgram: unsupported member-call statement
//   const g = f; inner(xs, g)        monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `f`
//   inner(xs, (a: T, b: T) => f(a, b))
//                                    emitProgram: only i32, i64, f64, f32, boolean,
//                                    struct, union, array, or string parameters are
//                                    supported
//
// WHAT DOES WORK, which is what makes this a hop and not a wall around generics:
//
//   - generic -> generic with a plain `T[]` argument:
//         function inner<T>(xs: T[]) { xs.length }
//         function outer<T>(xs: T[]) { inner(xs) }              // fine
//   - a NON-generic caller forwarding a CONCRETE closure into a generic function:
//         function outer(xs: i32[], f: (i32, i32) => boolean) { inner(xs, f) }   // fine
//
// So it is specifically the generic-to-generic FUNCTION-VALUE hop that has no lowering:
// the monomorphizer can substitute `T` into a list type it is passing along, and it can
// accept a closure whose type is already concrete, but it cannot carry a closure whose
// type still mentions the caller's own type parameter.
//
// The checker is happy — `vl check` reports this file clean at every severity. It is an
// EMIT-tier refusal, which is the honest kind: it names the parameter and the callee, and
// `vl check --codegen` sees it too. That is strictly better than the literal-union case
// next door (`xfail-miscompile-array-litunion-element.vl`), which emits an invalid module
// instead of refusing.
//
// Related, and not the same: `tests/cases/generics/error-genapp-tyaram-*.vl` pin the
// monomorphizer refusing a type-parameter ARRAY as an argument. This one is about a
// function VALUE.
function inner<T>(xs: T[], f: (T, T) => boolean) { f(xs[0], xs[1]) }

function outer<T>(xs: T[], f: (T, T) => boolean) { inner(xs, f) }

print(outer([1, 2], (a: i32, b: i32) => a < b))
